      "problem": "Rationalize $\\frac{10p}{\\sqrt{5q}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{5q}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{5q},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10p}{\\sqrt{5q}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10p}{\\sqrt{5q}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5q}}{\\sqrt{5q}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10p\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}}{\\sqrt{5q}\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10p\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}}{5q} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2p\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}}{\\cancel{5}\\cdot q} & = \\\\ \\frac{2p\\sqrt{5q}}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
